    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d609">

                <pb facs="0739" n="637" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg637"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.516/82: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Reinsch</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-08-07T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 7, 1919,
                                5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your July 31, 4 p.m.</p>
                <p>Department has transmitted to Bruce<anchor n="61" xml:id="d609-f1"/> telegrams in
                    question and has shown copies of telegrams and despatches to Denby<anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d609-f2"/> personally.</p>
                <pb facs="0740" n="638" xml:id="pg638"/>
                <p>Department approves encouragement of American enterprise, but cannot commit
                    itself with respect to specific cases until it has had opportunity to scrutinize
                    the actual terms of the contract. Nor can the Department well assume
                    responsibility for the good faith of the contracting parties.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d609-f1" n="61" xml:id="d609-f1-content">
                <p>Edward B. Bruce, President, Pacific Development Corp.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d609-f2" n="62" xml:id="d609-f2-content">
                <p>Charles Denby, Special Assistant to the Department.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
